        [Turn 6 REASONING] (3604 chars)
        Let  

\[
N=9973 ,\qquad 1\le x,y\le N .
\]

For a selected set \(S\) of lattice points write  

\[
r_i =\#\{(x,i)\in S\}\qquad (i=1,\dots ,N),\qquad   
c_j =\#\{(j,y)\in S\}\qquad (j=1,\dots ,N).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound

If a row \(i\) contains \(r_i\) points, it contributes  

\[
\binom{r_i}{2}= \frac{r_i(r_i-1)}2
\]

horizontal pairs.  
All horizontal pairs must have different lengths, otherwise the two
pairs would be the bases of an isosceles trapezoid with a horizontal
base.  
Hence the total number of horizontal pairs satisfies  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{N}\binom{r_i}{2}\le N-1 .\tag{1}
\]

(The same argument applied to the columns gives  

\[
\sum_{j=1}^{N}\binom{c_j}{2}\le N-1 .\tag{2}
\])

Now add the two inequalities.  Using  

\[
\sum r_i =\sum c_j =|S|=:M,
\qquad 
\sum\binom{r_i}{2}= \frac{\sum r_i^{2}-M}{2},
\]

(1) gives  

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{N} r_i^{2}\le 2(N-1)+M .
\]

By Cauchy–Schwarz, \(\displaystyle\sum r_i^{2}\ge\frac{M^{2}}{N}\); therefore  

\[
\frac{M^{2}}{N}\le M+2(N-1)
\Longrightarrow M^{2}-NM-2N(N-1)\le0 .
\]

Solving this quadratic for \(M\) yields  

\[
M\le\frac{N+\sqrt{9N^{2}-8N}}2
      =2N-1 .
\]

Thus **no selection can contain more than \(2N-1\) points**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A construction attaining the bound

The bound is tight.  
We construct a set of exactly \(2N-1\) points.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 2.1  A graceful ordering of the rows and columns  

Because a path on \(N\) vertices is graceful, there exists a permutation
\((r_1,r_2,\dots ,r_N)\) of \(\{1,\dots ,N\}\) such that the
adjacent differences  

\[
|r_{i+1}-r_i|\;(i=1,\dots ,N-1)
\]

are the distinct numbers \(1,2,\dots ,N-1\).
For odd \(N\) (our case) a simple choice is  

\[
r_1=1,\; r_2=N,\; r_3=2,\; r_4=N-1,\; r_5=3,\; r_6=N-2,\dots
\]

i.e.  

\[
r=(1,N,2,N-1,3,N-2,\ldots ).
\]

Take the same permutation for the columns:

\[
c_i=r_i\qquad (i=1,\dots ,N).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 2.2  The selected points  

Define  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A_i &= (r_i ,c_i) \qquad (i=1,\dots ,N),\\[2mm]
B_i &= (r_{i+1},c_i) \qquad (i=1,\dots ,N-1).
\end{aligned}
\]

The set  

\[
S=\{A_1,\dots ,A_N\}\cup\{B_1,\dots ,B_{N-1}\}
\]

contains exactly \(N+(N-1)=2N-1\) lattice points.

*Row structure.*  
Row \(r_i\;(i>1)\) contains the two points \(A_i\) and \(B_{i-1}\);
their horizontal distance is \(|c_i-c_{i-1}|\), which equals the
\(i-1\)‑st adjacent difference of the column permutation.
Since those differences are all distinct, every row that has two
points has a unique horizontal distance; the first row has only one
point. Consequently no two rows contain a pair of points with the same
horizontal distance, so no isosceles trapezoid with a horizontal base
can be formed.

*Column structure.*  
Column \(c_i\;(i<N)\) contains the two points \(A_i\) and \(B_i\);
their vertical distance is \(|r_{i+1}-r_i|\), the \(i\)‑th adjacent
difference of the row permutation, again all distinct. Hence no two
columns share a vertical distance, and a trapezoid with a vertical base
is impossible.

Thus the set \(S\) satisfies the required condition and has
\(2N-1\) points.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Result for \(N=9973\)

With \(N=9973\),

\[
\boxed{\,2N-1 = 2\cdot 9973-1 = 19945\,}
\]

lattice points can be selected, and this number is maximal.  

Hence the maximum possible number of lattice points is **19945**.

