        The optimal strategy when exactly two dice are re‑thrown is to keep the die whose value \(x\) gives the largest probability  

\[
\Pr\bigl(x+Y_1+Y_2=7\bigr)=\frac{\#\{(Y_1,Y_2):Y_1+Y_2=7-x\}}{36},
\qquad Y_1,Y_2\in\{1,\dots ,6\}.
\]

For a kept value \(x\) the number of favourable pairs \((Y_1,Y_2)\) is  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
x & \#\text{ways} \\ \hline
1 & 5\\
2 & 4\\
3 & 3\\
4 & 2\\
5 & 1\\
6 & 0
\end{array}
\]

Thus the success probability after keeping a die showing \(x\) is \(\frac{w_x}{36}\) where
\(w_1=5,w_2=4,w_3=3,w_4=2,w_5=1,w_6=0\).

For each initial triple \((a,b,c)\) Kevin keeps the die with the largest \(w\)-value, giving a conditional success probability \(\frac{\max\{w_a,w_b,w_c\}}{36}\).

Summing over all \(6^3=216\) possible initial triples,

\[
\sum_{a,b,c}\max\{w_a,w_b,w_c\}=855.
\]

Hence

\[
p=\frac{1}{216}\cdot\frac{855}{36}=\frac{95}{864},
\qquad
216p=\frac{95}{4}=23.75.
\]

