f(s)=\begin{cases}
1,2,3,4,5,6,5,4,3,2,1 & (s=2,\dots ,12)
\end{cases}
\]

which decreases as \(x\) increases.  

Let \(M\) be the minimum of the three initial dice.  
Then the success probability for a given initial roll is \(\dfrac{f(7-M)}{36}\).

The distribution of \(M\) for three independent fair dice is  

\[
P(M=k)=\Bigl(\frac{7-k}{6}\Bigr)^{\!3}-\Bigl(\frac{6-k}{6}\Bigr)^{\!3},
\qquad k=1,\dots ,6 .
\]

Hence the expected number of favourable ordered pairs for the two re‑thrown dice is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
E[f(7-M)]&=5\cdot\frac{91}{216}+4\cdot\frac{61}{216}
