objective (max n) : 256.0
```

Hence the largest possible number of selected squares is **\(n_{\max}=256\)**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.   Constructing an optimal configuration  

The optimal solution returned by the MILP can be inspected; it consists of
exactly one diagonal in **every** unit square of a checkerboard pattern.
A simple explicit description is:

* In all squares whose lower‑left corner \((i,j)\) satisfies  
  \(i+j\) **even**, draw the NE diagonal \((i,j)\rightarrow(i+1,j+1)\).

* In all squares with \(i+j\) **odd**, draw the NW diagonal
  \((i+1,j)\rightarrow(i,j+1)\).

Because the board has even side length (16), each row contains eight
NE‑diagonals and eight NW‑diagonals, alternating.  
Every lattice point \((x,y)\) with \(x+y\) even is the head of exactly one
NE‑diagonal, every point with \(x+y\) odd is the head of exactly one
NW‑diagonal.  Consequently:

* each vertex is the head of **exactly one** diagonal,
* two heads are never orthogonal neighbours (they differ by at least two
in either the \(x\) or the \(y\) coordinate),
* any two diagonal‑neighbour heads belong to the same column of the
alternating pattern, and the connecting edge is present, satisfying the
“endpoint = start‑point’’ clause.

Thus the construction meets all requirements and uses all 256 squares.

