For the \(3\times3\) grid the only admissible colourings are the two linear ones (up to a global additive constant modulo 3). The same holds for larger rectangles; the recurrence together with the “all‑different’’ requirement forces the residues to be globally either  

\[
c(x,y)\equiv x+2y\pmod 3\qquad\text{or}\qquad
c(x,y)\equiv 2x+y\pmod 3 .
\]

---

### From residues to the full function \(g\)

Write \(g(x,y)=\ell(x,y)+3k\) where \(\ell\) is one of the two linear solutions
\[
\ell_1(x,y)=x+2y,\qquad \ell_2(x,y)=2x+y,
\]
and \(k\) is a non‑negative integer constant.  
If the three values of an L‑shape are consecutive for \(\ell\), adding the same multiple of 3 to all three keeps them consecutive; adding different multiples would create a gap of at least 3, which is impossible. Hence the only freedom beyond the linear part is a global shift by a multiple of 3.

Thus every admissible function has the form  

\[
g(x,y)=x+2y+3k\quad\text{or}\quad g(x,y)=2x+y+3k,
\qquad k\in\mathbb N_0 .
\]

---

### Value at \((4000,4036)\)

